1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,500
At the Moon's south pole, the Sun never climbs.

2
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:14,500
It circles the horizon, so a single ridge can put a lander
in the dark for days and cut its line to Earth.

3
00:00:15,500 --> 00:00:22,000
We are [Team name]: [Name], [Name], [Name] and [Name].

4
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:29,500
Our challenge is the CLPS Lunar Mission Browser.
Our answer is PolarWindow.

5
00:00:30,500 --> 00:00:36,500
NASA's commercial lunar deliveries, CLPS, are public missions.

6
00:00:36,500 --> 00:00:44,000
But their data is scattered: flight paths in one archive,
terrain in another, ground stations in a handbook.

7
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:50,500
A lander team has one question first. Is this landing site,
on these dates, workable for my lander?

8
00:00:50,500 --> 00:00:54,500
Today that takes a specialist and days of work.

9
00:00:55,500 --> 00:01:00,000
PolarWindow brings it together.
It browses every CLPS delivery,

10
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:09,500
and it works out, hour by hour, what a lander gets at any site:
sunlight, contact with Earth, and the gaps in between.

11
00:01:10,500 --> 00:01:18,500
Here are all twenty CLPS deliveries, flown and planned. Every fact
links to its source, and what is not published says so.

12
00:01:18,500 --> 00:01:23,500
Each mission follows six steps,
from getting ready to coming home.

13
00:01:24,500 --> 00:01:33,000
This is IM-2 on the surface. Four numbers: time in sunlight, time in contact
with Earth, the longest night, and the longest time without contact.

14
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:37,500
Each carries its margin.

15
00:01:38,500 --> 00:01:44,000
The reason is the skyline.

16
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:51,500
As the dates play, the Sun and Earth move along the hills
around the site, and every number is worked out again.

17
00:01:52,500 --> 00:01:58,500
Compare candidate sites side by side, one measure at a time.

18
00:01:58,500 --> 00:02:05,500
When two are too close to call, it says so.
There is no score and no best site.

19
00:02:06,500 --> 00:02:12,500
A team can add its own mission by dropping in a brief,
then try a what-if.

20
00:02:12,500 --> 00:02:19,500
Assumed values are always hatched,
so nobody mistakes them for facts.

21
00:02:20,500 --> 00:02:24,000
And you can simply ask.

22
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:34,500
The assistant may only state numbers its tools returned,
and it shows how it got each one.

23
00:02:35,500 --> 00:02:38,500
PolarWindow is built on NASA open data.

24
00:02:38,500 --> 00:02:45,500
The LOLA laser height maps from the Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter
give every site its skyline.

25
00:02:45,500 --> 00:02:53,000
NASA's SPICE kernels and the CLPS trajectory archive give the positions
of the Sun, Earth and each spacecraft.

26
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:57,500
Deep Space Network horizon masks say when a dish can listen.

27
00:02:57,500 --> 00:03:04,500
DONKI gives space weather, GRAIL the Moon's gravity,
and Diviner the surface temperatures.

28
00:03:05,500 --> 00:03:11,000
We checked the models against missions that have flown,
using their reconstructed trajectories.

29
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:15,000
More than fourteen hundred automated tests guard the code.

30
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:24,500
Every number says whether it was worked out, published,
entered or assumed, and how far it could be off.

31
00:03:25,500 --> 00:03:32,000
For lander and payload teams, the answer is on one page
instead of spread across archives.

32
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:39,500
For students and the public, it opens a real mission's choices
to anyone with a browser.

33
00:03:40,500 --> 00:03:49,500
PolarWindow. Open source, and live at clpsspaceapps.ahbab.dev.
We are [Team name]. Thank you.
